; A key the compiler knows is never a near miss of another one.
;
; `pre` is one insertion from `pure`, and until 2026-08-29 every
; `;@axiom:pre(...)` drew AX3039 "did you mean `pure`?" - measured. The
; near-miss test now asks first whether the key is one the compiler
; knows; `pre`, `post`, `restrict` and `unhandled` are, so the four
; below draw nothing - and `unhandled` is checked on an `effect`
; declaration, which is the only declaration kind whose tags are
; walked for this at all (`checkEffectDeclAxtags`), so its silence here
; is evidence about that walk and not only about the key set.
; What still fires is a slip FROM a known key: `restrct`
; suggests `restrict` - a suggestion that did not exist before, so a
; compiler without the change fails this case in both directions - and
; `pur` suggests `pure` as it always did, which is the control.
;@axiom:pre((> n 0))
(:: guarded (-> Int Int))

(fn (guarded n) n)

;@axiom:post((> result 0))
(:: ensured (-> Int Int))

(fn (ensured n) n)

;@axiom:restrict(no-io)
(:: confined (-> Int Int))

(fn (confined n) n)

;@axiom:unhandled(trap)
(effect Declared
  (announce :: (-> String Int)))

;@axiom:restrct(no-io)
(:: slipped (-> Int Int))

(fn (slipped n) n)

;@axiom:pur
(:: control (-> Int Int))

(fn (control n) n)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (cast Int (handle (announce "x") (Declared) (lambda (s) 0))))
